Warts - Hands (verruca vulgaris) 
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Hyperkeratotic verrucous papules, Disruption of dermatoglyphics, Thrombosed capillaries/black dots

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-050
Reason for Visit: Sudden, continuous bleeding and localized pain from a chronic, rough nodule on the hand after accidental trauma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a pediatric patient experiencing acute, continuous bleeding from a rough, raised bump on the finger after it was snagged during play. The parents were highly distressed as the bleeding would not stop with basic tissue pressure. Urgent assessment was required to achieve mechanical hemostasis, formally assess the lesion to rule out an aggressive vascular tumor (e.g., pyogenic granuloma), and provide immediate clinical reassurance before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He caught this hard, rough bump on his finger while playing, and it just won't stop bleeding. It's bleeding much more than a normal cut."
History of Present Illness: The 8-year-old patient has had multiple hyperkeratotic exophytic papules on the dorsal fingers for the past 6 months. The lesions have a fleshy, greyish colour and a rough verrucous surface texture. They have been entirely asymptomatic until today. An hour ago, the patient accidentally snagged the largest nodule on the right index finger against a door frame. This caused a partial avulsion and sudden, continuous point bleeding. The parents note the presence of multiple similar lesions due to apparent autoinoculation from scratching. There is a complete absence of systemic inflammatory symptoms. The persistent bleeding and the child's distress prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Independent pediatric patient.
Social/Safety Concerns: Parental anxiety regarding uncontrolled bleeding.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 28 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing child, slightly tearful, holding the finger tightly wrapped in a bloody tissue.
Physical Exam: On the dorsal surface of the right index finger, there is a 6 mm hyperkeratotic, verrucous papule. The superficial layer has been traumatically torn, revealing a highly vascular base that is actively oozing blood. Crucially, there is a clear disruption of normal dermatoglyphics across the intact portion of the lesion, and visible thrombosed capillaries (black dots) are present at the base of the torn tissue. Several smaller, intact verrucous papules are present on the adjacent middle finger. There is no surrounding cellulitic erythema, induration, or purulence.
Environmental Observations: Normal home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Verruca Vulgaris (Common Viral Wart) - Status: Acute mechanical trauma and hemorrhage
Differentials:

Pyogenic Granuloma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of multiple lesions, the chronic history (6 months), and the distinctly hyperkeratotic, verrucous surface texture of the intact lesions. Pyogenic granulomas are typically solitary, rapidly growing, smooth, and friable red nodules.

Molluscum Contagiosum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rough, verrucous surface rather than a smooth, pearly, dome-shaped papule with central umbilication.

Squamous Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pediatric age demographic, the multiplicity of the lesions, and the classic warty morphology heavily favoring a benign HPV infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The pediatric patient presents with an actively bleeding, traumatized verruca vulgaris. Viral warts have a highly vascularized core (evident as "black dots" or thrombosed capillaries), which bleed profusely when the hyperkeratotic roof is mechanically avulsed. Triage accurately flagged the continuous bleeding and parental panic as an urgent scenario requiring intervention. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was vital to achieve definitive hemostasis using chemical cautery or firm pressure dressing, which the parents were unable to accomplish. By correctly diagnosing the benign viral etiology and immediately stopping the bleeding, the intervention successfully de-escalated the acute family distress and prevented an unnecessary emergency department visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Achieved complete hemostasis utilizing firm direct pressure and the precise application of a silver nitrate stick to the bleeding vascular core. Secured a firm, non-adherent protective dressing around the digit.
Positive Behavioral Change: By leaving this firm bandage completely dry and untouched for the next 48 hours, you will protect the delicate, healing blood vessels, absolutely preventing the scary bleeding from starting again. By strictly avoiding biting your fingernails or chewing on the bumps, you will stop the virus from spreading to your lips and other fingers.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to assess the healed lesion and discuss definitive treatment options (e.g., topical salicylic acid or cryotherapy) for the remaining warts.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the bandage becomes completely soaked with blood and continues dripping despite 15 minutes of firm, tight pressure, or if the finger becomes deeply red, hot, and swollen with pus, present to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Warts are highly vascular, meaning they bleed easily when picked. The bleeding is harmless once controlled.
Clinical Course: The traumatized wart will form a dark scab due to the silver nitrate and heal over the next week. The warts themselves will require specific treatment to completely disappear.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Apply firm, uninterrupted pressure around the finger if the bleeding restarts. Do not peel off the dressing prematurely.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A very common skin virus has caused these rough bumps. They have a rich blood supply, which is why it bled so much when he scraped it. The chemical stick we used sealed the broken blood vessels instantly.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to cut, clip, or file the warts at home. Cover any new warts with a simple band-aid during school to prevent spreading the virus to other children.

